## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer mechanisms, wakeup latency, and concrete data structures suitable for embedded or real-time operating systems.

## Key Concepts
- Timers with absolute expiry and optional periodicity
- Heap-based scheduling for efficient expiry checks
- Interrupt-safe wakeups and drift prevention
- Latency testing with mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
function timer_add(heap, expiry, period, task) {
  push(heap, { expiry, period, task });
  siftUp(heap, heap.length - 1);
}

function timer_interrupt(now_ms) {
  while (heap.length > 0 && heap[0].expiry <= now_ms) {
    const timer = popMin(heap);
    timer.task();
    if (timer.period > 0) {
      timer.expiry = now_ms + timer.period;
      push(heap, timer);
    }
  }
}
```

## Testing Considerations
Run mixed CPU-bound and timer-heavy workloads simultaneously. Measure wake latency (actual vs. expected wake time) and jitter (variance in wake intervals) to validate drift prevention and real-time guarantees.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of user-space vs kernel-space concept interplay, synchronization primitives, and designing a lazy I/O cache with proper wakeups.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block state machine (EMPTY, PENDING, LOADED, ERROR)
- Fast path vs slow path with contention handling
- Wait queues (futex/condvar) and wakeup semantics
- Avoiding thundering herd with CAS and double-checked locking
- Error propagation from I/O to callers

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  void *data;
  atomic_int state; // 0=EMPTY,1=PENDING,2=LOADED,3=ERROR
  int waiters;
  pthread_mutex_t mutex;
  pthread_cond_t cond;
} cache_entry;

#define EMPTY 0
#define PENDING 1
#define LOADED 2
#define ERROR 3

void* block_read(int block_id) {
  cache_entry *entry = &cache[block_id];

  // Fast path
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    return entry->data;
  }

  pthread_mutex_lock(&entry->mutex);

  // Double-check after acquiring lock
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);
    return entry->data;
  }

  // Transition to PENDING if first thread
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&entry->state, &EMPTY, PENDING)) {
    enqueue_io_request(block_id);
  }

  // Block until I/O completes
  while (atomic_load(&entry->state) == PENDING) {
    entry->waiters++;
    pthread_cond_wait(&entry->cond, &entry->mutex);
    entry->waiters--;
  }

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);

  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == ERROR) {
    return NULL; // Propagate error
  }

  return entry->data;
}
```

